insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('ec083ec5-1b46-53fa-99e0-1d9a63df7dd1'::uuid,'f379ea8e-ae82-5eb6-9887-f700227215ce'::uuid,'v1.2 working draft (IAT v1.5)',E'**CHAPTER 2  \n**\n\n**CDRRMO ORGANIZATIONAL STRUCTURE,  \nSTAFFING AND HUMAN RESOURCE MANAGEMENT**\n\n**  \n**\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)  \nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*  \nAssessment-informed working draft  \nSubject to institutional, HR, budgetary and legal validation*\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Document attribute** | **Control information**                                                                                  |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document title         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 2                                                   |\n| Chapter title          | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management                                  |\n| Document status        | Working draft for technical and institutional validation                                                 |\n| Document owner         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                       |\n| Technical custodian    | CDRRMO Head / designated MOO focal person                                                                |\n| Reviewing offices      | City Legal Office; HRMO; Budget Office; CDRRMO; OCD Region VIII; other offices as applicable             |\n| Version / date         | Draft 1.2 / 6 November 2026                                                                              |\n| Approval authority     | To be determined under the City''s document-control and approval arrangements                             |\n| Review cycle           | At least annually and following material changes in law, structure, staffing or operational requirements |\n\n## REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | **Prepared / reviewed by** | **Approval** |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|--------------|\n| 0.1         | 31 Oct 2026 | Initial assessment-informed Chapter 2 draft                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n| 1.1         | 6 Nov 2026  | Updated to IAT v1.5: October 2026 FGD, KIIs and site visits; 2021–2024 City issuances; A2 and B4 findings; priority actions aligned to the workbook Action Plan.                                                                                                     | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n| 1.2         | 6 Nov 2026  | Added the structure and staffing assessment (6.3), EOC staffing at Red (10.1), the cluster-based barangay coordinator proposal (10.2), the budget-cycle RACI (2.16.2), the Brgy. Liloan site visit, the Geo-COP staffing note, and APA-captioned figures and tables. | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction and Rationale\n\nThe effectiveness of Ormoc City''s disaster risk reduction and management system depends on a permanent, competent and adequately supported institutional workforce. Laws, plans, facilities, information systems and response assets produce results only when responsibilities are assigned to qualified personnel, authority is understood, work is coordinated and continuity is maintained during periods of heightened risk and emergency operations.\n\nChapter 1 established the legal and governance relationships among the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council (CDRRMC), the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO), the Emergency Operations Center (EOC), and the Incident Command System (ICS). This chapter translates that governance framework into an organizational and human-resource operating model. It defines the CDRRMO''s functional structure, division-level responsibilities, staffing principles, position accountability, workforce development, personnel welfare and arrangements for supporting an activated EOC.\n\nThe chapter treats organizational capacity as a system property. It is not measured solely by the number of positions or the existence of an organizational chart. It depends on the alignment of mandates, staffing, competencies, supervision, work processes, information flows, equipment, funding and inter-office relationships. A position is operationally meaningful only when its mandate, authority, expected outputs, reporting relationship, required competencies and performance measures are clear.\n\n![Figure 1: 1. Introduction and Rationale](/library/ch2v12/fig-01.webp)\n\nThe October 2026 Institutional Assessment identifies the CDRRMO''s organizational structure and human-resource arrangements as a priority improvement area. The A2 domain tests alignment with Joint Memorandum Circular (JMC) No. 2014-1, the permanent status and qualifications of the CDRRMO Head, minimum staffing, position descriptions, 24/7 operational capability, competency-based training and personnel welfare. The EOC B4 domain separately tests the EOC''s incident-time organizational structure, pre-designated personnel and alternates, duty rosters, liaison desks, job action sheets and welfare arrangements. These are related but distinct workforce systems.\n\nThe assessment is diagnostic. It does not itself establish legal non-compliance, approve a staffing pattern or create positions. All staffing and organizational proposals remain subject to verification of the current legal framework, approved City organizational structure, plantilla, qualification standards, appropriations and applicable Civil Service Commission (CSC) and Department of Budget and Management (DBM) rules.\n\n## 1.1 Assessment Findings for this Chapter (IAT v1.5)\n\nThe findings below come from the Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.5 (6 November 2026). The October 2026 assessment combined a focus group discussion with the CDRRMO Head and staff at the CDRRM Office, which houses the OpCen and the EOC; key informant interviews with the CDRRMO Chiefs of Operations, Research and Planning, and Administration, the CPDO, the CSWDO and the City Administrator''s Office; site visits to the EOC and the Operations and Rescue Office; and a visit to Brgy. Liloan, one of the flood-prone barangays, with an established evacuation site, evacuation protocol, and camp coordination and management arrangements. The team also reviewed City plans, 2021–2025 issuances, the CDRRMO organizational chart and internal staff papers.\n\n**Stakeholder position.** All offices consulted share one aim: clear roles and responsibilities for each unit and accountable person, and that these are carried out. All agree that a Manual of Operations will help significantly. This chapter turns that aim into approved structures, positions, rosters and records.\n\n**Table 2.1**\n\n*Maturity and Compliance of the Chapter 2 Domains, IAT v1.5*\n\n| **Domain**                                                    | **Maturity index (0–4)** | **Band**   | **Mandatory compliance** | **High-priority gaps** |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|------------|--------------------------|------------------------|\n| A2 Organization, Staffing and Human Resources (10 indicators) | 2.00                     | Developing | 14.3% (1 of 7)           | 4                      |\n| B4 EOC Organization and Staffing (6 indicators)               | 1.31                     | Initial    | 0% (0 of 4)              | 2                      |\n\n*Note.* Maturity index on a 0–4 scale. Mandatory compliance is the share of scored mandatory indicators rated 3 or 4.\n\nScores follow the evidence rule in Methodology 5.2: interviews alone support a maximum score of 2; a 3 needs an approved document and proof of use; a 4 adds a test, exercise or after-action review.\n\n**Table 2.2**\n\n*Chapter 2 Indicators, Findings and Responses (IAT v1.5)*\n\n| **IAT ref** | **Score** | **Priority** | **Finding (IAT v1.5)**                                                                                                                                                                                    | **Chapter response (section)** |\n|-------------|-----------|--------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| A1.4        | 3         | Low          | CDRRMO acts as CDRRMC Secretariat; no written secretariat procedure; records lapses.                                                                                                                      | 7.2; Chapter 1 GOV-02          |\n| A2.1        | 2         | High         | Sections exist, but not as an approved JMC 2014-1 structure; normal-operations and response tasks are mixed.                                                                                              | 6; 7                           |\n| A2.2        | 4         | Low          | The head-of-office post is permanent. Qualification records and the succession order are still to be documented.                                                                                          | 9.2; 11.2                      |\n| A2.3        | 2         | High         | Filled-to-required ratio not proven. The 10-Year Plan lists 10 planned plantilla items (4 + 4 + 2).                                                                                                       | 8                              |\n| A2.4        | 2         | Medium       | No position description for each post; Admin functions bypassed in some activities.                                                                                                                       | 9; Annex 2-A                   |\n| A2.5        | 2         | High         | Site visit: 24-hour duty runs for City Traffic Monitoring, Public Safety and Emergency Monitoring. No approved OpCen SOP, roster or handover record; no written split of tasks between the three streams. | 11.1; Annex 2-E                |\n| A2.6        | 1         | Medium       | No training needs analysis or competency-based training plan.                                                                                                                                             | 12                             |\n| A2.7        | 2         | Medium       | CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03/04 s.2022 approved a basis for volunteer insurance; no policy sighted. Nothing for regular staff (insurance, hazard pay, PPE, MHPSS).                                                 | 13                             |\n| A2.8        | 3         | Low          | ACDV accreditation and a partner MOA exist and are used; no registry, deployment or insurance rules; tasking during activation unclear.                                                                   | 14.3                           |\n| A2.9        | 0         | High         | No anticipatory-action focal person designated (RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 1).                                                                                                                             | 7.1                            |\n| A2.10       | 1         | Low          | No skills inventory across the CDRRMO, partner offices and barangays.                                                                                                                                     | 12                             |\n| B4.1        | 1         | High         | EOC structure with the five NDRRMC Memo 131 functions not defined.                                                                                                                                        | 10                             |\n| B4.2        | 1         | High         | Alternates not named; a 72-hour activation cannot be staffed two deep.                                                                                                                                    | 10; 11.2; Annex 2-D            |\n| B4.3        | 2         | Medium       | Site visit: 24-hour duty observed. Roster and handover protocol not sighted.                                                                                                                              | 11.1; Annex 2-E                |\n| B4.4        | 2         | Medium       | Liaison desks not written down.                                                                                                                                                                           | 10                             |\n| B4.5        | 1         | Medium       | No job action sheets for EOC roles.                                                                                                                                                                       | 10                             |\n| B4.6        | 1         | Low          | No written rest cycles, meals or MHPSS for EOC staff.                                                                                                                                                     | 11.3; 13                       |\n\n*Note.* Priority is the workbook''s gap-priority band: (4 − score) × weight, × 1.5 for mandatory items; High is 9 or more.\n\n# 2. Purpose and Objectives\n\nThis chapter establishes a clear, legally grounded and implementable organizational and human-resource framework for the CDRRMO and its support to the EOC.\n\nSpecifically, it aims to:\n\n1.  Translate the mandates established in Chapter 1 into a functional organizational structure aligned with RA No. 10121 and JMC No. 2014-1.\n\n2.  Define the responsibilities, authority, outputs and interfaces of the CDRRMO Head and its core functional divisions.\n\n3.  Establish principles for staffing establishment, plantilla management, position descriptions, succession and workforce continuity.\n\n4.  Clarify the distinction between permanent CDRRMO positions and personnel assigned or designated to support the EOC during activation.\n\n5.  Provide a systematic approach to competency assessment, training, certification, exercises and professional development.\n\n6.  Establish duty-of-care arrangements for staff and responders, including occupational safety, insurance, protective equipment, rest, mental health and psychosocial support.\n\n7.  Strengthen performance management, supervision, records, accountability and continuous improvement.\n\n8.  Provide operational workflows, RACI assignments, forms and evidence requirements that can be implemented and audited.\n\n# 3. Scope and Application\n\nThis chapter applies to the CDRRMO as a permanent office of the City Government and to City personnel formally assigned, designated or mobilized to support its functions. It also establishes the personnel interface with the EOC, without replacing the detailed EOC activation, incident management and operational procedures in subsequent chapters.\n\n| **Institutional actor**                          | **Application under this chapter**                                                                                                                                           |\n|--------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| City Mayor / Local Chief Executive               | Executive leadership; approval of organizational and personnel actions within legal authority; designation and mobilization of personnel as permitted by law and City rules. |\n| CDRRMC                                           | Policy oversight, review of institutional capability and endorsement of DRRM capacity priorities; does not administer individual personnel matters.                          |\n| CDRRMO Head                                      | Office leadership, functional integration, workforce planning, supervision, performance and institutional accountability.                                                    |\n| Administration and Training Division             | Administrative services, personnel coordination, training administration, records and office support within approved structure.                                              |\n| Research and Planning Division                   | Risk-informed planning, policy and technical analysis, institutional planning, monitoring inputs and knowledge support.                                                      |\n| Operations and Warning Division                  | Operational readiness, warning and preparedness functions, response coordination support and duty officer arrangements.                                                      |\n| EOC Manager / designated EOC staff               | Incident-time coordination roles assigned under the EOC organizational structure and activation procedures.                                                                  |\n| HRMO / Budget / Accounting / Legal Office        | Specialist review and administration of personnel, position, budgetary, legal and records requirements.                                                                      |\n| City departments, barangays and partner agencies | Provide designated liaison personnel and technical support within their respective mandates.                                                                                 |\n\nThe chapter covers normal operations, heightened preparedness and emergency operations. It does not authorize the CDRRMO to exercise powers assigned to other City offices, national government agencies, utilities, professional regulators or incident commanders.\n\n![Figure 2: 3. Scope and Application](/library/ch2v12/fig-02.webp)\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Administrative Framework\n\nOrganizational and personnel arrangements shall be implemented in accordance with the hierarchy of laws, rules and approved City instruments described in Chapter 1. The following references are particularly relevant to this chapter:\n\n| **Instrument / framework**                                        | **Relevance to organizational and HR arrangements**                                                                                       |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA No. 10121 (2010), particularly Sections 12 and 14              | Establishes the LDRRMO and its functions, including the Head and the functional areas; provides for DRRM capacity development.            |\n| IRR of RA No. 10121                                               | Operational details for local DRRM councils and offices; exact provisions to be checked against the official current text.                |\n| NDRRMC–DILG–DBM–CSC JMC No. 2014-1                                | Guidance on LDRRMO organizational structure, functions, staffing and personnel arrangements.                                              |\n| RA No. 7160 (1991), Local Government Code                         | Local government authority, organization, personnel and administrative arrangements, subject to national law.                             |\n| CSC laws, rules and qualification standards                       | Appointment, qualification, position classification, performance management, training, conduct and personnel administration.              |\n| DBM rules and approved staffing pattern                           | Position creation, classification, compensation, plantilla and budget authorization.                                                      |\n| RA No. 11036 (2018), Mental Health Act                            | Mental health promotion, access to support and protection relevant to personnel and responders.                                           |\n| RA No. 10173 (2012), Data Privacy Act                             | Protection of personnel, health, emergency contact and other personal information.                                                        |\n| RA No. 9710, RA No. 7277 as amended, RA No. 9994 and RA No. 10821 | Gender-responsive, disability-inclusive, older-person-sensitive and child-sensitive institutional and operational arrangements.           |\n| ICS guidance and applicable NDRRMC/OCD EOC guidance               | Role-based incident organization and EOC staffing; exact current EOC guidance and issuance status shall be verified with OCD Region VIII. |\n\nWhere a local organizational chart, staffing proposal, designation or SOP conflicts with a statute, national rule or approved plantilla, the higher legal or administrative authority shall prevail. This chapter does not independently create plantilla positions, authorize compensation, confer professional licenses or amend the City''s approved organizational structure.\n\n![Figure 3: 4. Legal, Policy and Administrative Framework](/library/ch2v12/fig-03.webp)\n\n# 5. Organizational Design Principles\n\n| **Principle**                                   | **Operational application**                                                                                                         |\n|-------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Legality and mandate alignment                  | Every function and position must be traced to a lawful mandate, approved structure or authorized designation.                       |\n| Functional clarity                              | Each division and position must have defined responsibilities, deliverables, authority limits and reporting lines.                  |\n| Separation of governance and management         | CDRRMC provides statutory oversight; CDRRMO manages the permanent technical and administrative work.                                |\n| Separation of permanent and activated functions | The CDRRMO remains operational during normal periods; the EOC is configured and staffed according to activation requirements.       |\n| Scalability and interoperability                | Staffing and role assignments must expand or contract according to incident complexity and integrate with ICS and partner agencies. |\n| Continuity and redundancy                       | Critical positions require alternates, documented handover and succession arrangements.                                             |\n| Competency-based deployment                     | Personnel are assigned according to qualifications, training, demonstrated competence and role requirements.                        |\n| Inclusion and duty of care                      | Workforce arrangements must be safe, accessible, gender-responsive and attentive to fatigue and psychosocial needs.                 |\n| Evidence and performance                        | Organizational effectiveness must be demonstrated through approved records, work outputs, exercises and review.                     |\n\n![Figure 4: 5. Organizational Design Principles](/library/ch2v12/fig-04.webp)\n\nThe organizational model shall be proportionate to Ormoc City''s risk profile, geographic and sectoral diversity, service obligations and available resources. It shall not assume that a larger organizational chart automatically produces better performance. The objective is a fit-for-purpose structure with clear accountability and sufficient capability to discharge mandatory functions.\n\n# 6. CDRRMO Organizational Structure and Functional Architecture\n\nThe CDRRMO shall be headed by a qualified CDRRMO Head and organized around the three core functional areas identified in JMC No. 2014-1: (a) Administration and Training; (b) Research and Planning; and (c) Operations and Warning. The precise division names, unit configuration, staffing complement and reporting relationships shall follow the City''s approved organizational and staffing pattern and applicable national requirements.\n\nThe proposed functional architecture below is a functional reference model for MOO purposes. It is not, by itself, an approved organogram or authority to create offices or positions.\n\n| **Organizational level**           | **Core accountability**                                                                                                       | **Key interfaces**                                                        |\n|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| City Mayor / Local Chief Executive | Executive direction and lawful personnel / resource decisions                                                                 | CDRRMC; CDRRMO; HRMO; Budget; City departments                            |\n| CDRRMC                             | Policy oversight, coordination and review of institutional DRRM performance                                                   | Mayor; CDRRMO Secretariat; member offices; CSOs; private sector           |\n| CDRRMO Head                        | Overall management, technical leadership, integration and accountability for CDRRMO outputs                                   | Mayor; CDRRMC; all divisions; EOC Manager; HRMO                           |\n| Administration and Training        | Administrative support, personnel records coordination, training systems, office services and Secretariat support as assigned | HRMO; Budget; Accounting; procurement; records; CDRRMC Secretariat        |\n| Research and Planning              | Risk analysis, plans, policy integration, program development, monitoring and technical knowledge management                  | CPDO; sector offices; GIS/ICT; barangays; academic and technical partners |\n| Operations and Warning             | Operational readiness, warning coordination, preparedness, duty officer and response coordination functions                   | EOC; ICS/IMT; BDRRMCs; PAGASA; PHIVOLCS; response agencies                |\n\nThe CDRRMO Head may establish internal work teams, focal-person arrangements or task groups within the approved structure to address cross-cutting functions such as climate change adaptation, anticipatory action, inclusive DRRM, public service continuity, digital systems and partnership coordination. Such arrangements shall not be represented as permanent divisions or plantilla positions unless duly approved.\n\n## 6.1 Organizational Chart – Functional Reference\n\nCity Mayor / Local Chief Executive → CDRRMC (policy oversight and coordination) → CDRRMO Head (permanent office leadership) → three core functional divisions: Administration and Training; Research and Planning; Operations and Warning. The EOC is shown as an operational coordination mechanism supported by designated CDRRMO and inter-agency personnel when activated. It is not a fourth permanent CDRRMO division and does not replace ICS command.\n\n![Figure 5: 6.1 Organizational Chart – Functional Reference](/library/ch2v12/fig-05.webp)\n\n## 6.2 Management and Reporting Relationships\n\n- The CDRRMO Head reports through the City''s approved administrative and executive reporting arrangements.\n\n- Division chiefs report to the CDRRMO Head and are accountable for division work plans, outputs, staff supervision and records.\n\n- Personnel remain subject to the City''s personnel administration, supervision and performance management systems.\n\n- Personnel designated to an activated EOC retain their underlying employment and administrative relationships unless a lawful temporary assignment or detail provides otherwise.\n\n- Incident-specific operational coordination and tasking shall follow the approved EOC and ICS arrangements; it shall not be interpreted as a change in permanent appointment or plantilla.\n\n## 6.3 Current Structure Compared With the Recommended Model\n\nThe office chart reviewed during the assessment shows that every function RA 10121 Sec. 12(a) requires is already in place, carried by a committed workforce of about 47 people that includes a 24/7 medical and rescue team. The main structural issue is employment status: four posts are permanent, the rest are casual or job order, and two posts are vacant (Figure 2.1). Strengthening the structure is therefore mainly about securing the positions the office already relies on.\n\n**Figure 2.1**\n\n*CDRRMO Posts by Organizational Unit and Employment Status*\n\n![Figure 6: 6.3 Current Structure Compared With the Recommended Model](/library/ch2v12/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Counts from the CDRRMO organizational chart reviewed in October 2026; heads of sections are included in their units. The casual and job-order split is hard to read on the scanned chart and shall be confirmed against the Plantilla of Personnel. The chart predates recent staff movements and shall be updated.\n\n**Table 2.3**\n\n*Compliance Findings on the Current Organizational Structure*\n\n| **\\#** | **Standard**                                                                                    | **Finding**                                                                                                                                                                                                              | **IAT link (score)**         |\n|--------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------|\n| 1      | RA 10121 Sec. 12(a): administration and training; research and planning; operations and warning | All three functions are in place, arranged as four sections: training sits with operations, and warning and monitoring is a separate section. Reconcile the chart with the three-division model in section 7.            | A2.1 (2)                     |\n| 2      | JMC 2014-1 minimum plantilla                                                                    | Two of the four section-head posts are permanent, and two sections have no permanent post yet. Confirm the exact JMC 2014-1 items with the HRMO.                                                                         | A2.3 (2), A1.1 (2)           |\n| 3      | CDRRMO at department level                                                                      | No ordinance yet makes the CDRRMO a regular department. Confirm the head-of-office item and salary grade with the HRMO.                                                                                                  | A1.1 (2), A2.2 (4)           |\n| 4      | 24/7 OpCen with a documented roster                                                             | Seven OpCen staff cannot hold two per shift around the clock (Figure 2.2).                                                                                                                                               | A2.5 (2), B4.3 (2)           |\n| 5      | Research and planning capacity                                                                  | LDRRMP work, CDRA liaison, PDRA support, monitoring, GIS and data rest on a small team with no dedicated GIS, data or M&E post.                                                                                          | A5.6 (2), A10.4 (2)          |\n| 6      | Accountable functions                                                                           | Petty cash, supply and inventory control and procurement support sit in the Administrative Section. Confirm with the HRMO and the City Accountant that accountable roles are held by eligible staff (COA and CSC rules). | A4.8                         |\n| 7      | Posts the IAT expects                                                                           | No anticipatory-action focal person, public information and risk communication post, PDNA team or logistics and warehouse lead.                                                                                          | A2.9 (0), A6.5 (2), A9.1 (1) |\n| 8      | EOC structure                                                                                   | The chart shows peacetime sections only; the EOC structure, alternates and cluster desks are set out separately (Chapter 1, section 6.3).                                                                                | B4.1 (1), B4.2 (1)           |\n\n*Note.* Findings describe the structure, not the performance of any person. Medium-confidence items (3 and 6) shall be confirmed with the HRMO and the City Accountant.\n\n**Figure 2.2**\n\n*OpCen Staff in Post Compared With the Staff Needed for Two Per Shift*\n\n![Figure 7: 6.3 Current Structure Compared With the Recommended Model](/library/ch2v12/fig-07.webp)\n\n*Note.* Three shifts × two staff × seven days = 42 shifts a week. At about five shifts per person, about nine people are needed before leave and training are counted.\n\n**Recommended structure.** The assessment recommends keeping RA 10121''s three divisions (Figure 2.3):\n\n- Administration and Training takes over the training programme from Operations.\n\n- Operations and Warning holds two units: the OpCen and the rescue and medical team.\n\n- Every division has a permanent head, and the OpCen grows to at least nine staff so it can keep two per shift.\n\n- Research and Planning grows to at least four technical posts: planning and M&E, GIS and data, the anticipatory-action focal person, and a cluster-coordination lead (section 10.2). CDP 2024–2030 PPAs 1.1.1, 1.15.4 and 2.1.3 already call for regular positions, data posts and risk-communication posts.\n\n- The EOC keeps its own structure, separate from the office chart (Chapter 1, section 6.3).\n\n**Figure 2.3**\n\n*Recommended CDRRMO Structure Under RA 10121 and JMC 2014-1*\n\n![Figure 8: 6.3 Current Structure Compared With the Recommended Model](/library/ch2v12/fig-08.webp)\n\n*Note.* Proposed for validation at the December 2026 workshop. M&E = monitoring and evaluation.\n\n# 7. Functional Mandates and Division-Level Responsibilities\n\n## 7.1 CDRRMO Head\n\nThe CDRRMO Head provides overall leadership and is accountable for translating statutory mandates, CDRRMC policy direction and approved City plans into coordinated office work. Subject to the City''s approved structure and applicable rules, the Head shall:\n\n- Lead preparation and implementation of the CDRRMO annual work and financial plans, staffing proposals and performance commitments.\n\n- Direct and integrate the three core functional divisions and ensure that responsibilities are allocated without duplication.\n\n- Advise the City Mayor and CDRRMC on DRRM policy, institutional readiness, risk-informed priorities and capability gaps.\n\n- Ensure that the CDRRMO performs its statutory and assigned Secretariat functions and maintains reliable governance records.\n\n- Coordinate with CPDO and sector offices to mainstream DRR and CCA into development, land-use, investment and public-service continuity processes.\n\n- Ensure that risk information, preparedness, early warning, response coordination and recovery support are linked through documented processes.\n\n- Oversee readiness arrangements for the EOC, including pre-designated personnel, alternates, role descriptions and coordination with partner agencies.\n\n- Ensure compliance with approved personnel, procurement, financial, records, privacy, occupational safety and reporting requirements.\n\n- Lead performance review, after-action learning and corrective action within the CDRRMO''s authority.\n\n**Anticipatory-action focal person (A2.9).** The CDRRMO Head shall designate an anticipatory-action focal person by office order (RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 1; Rule VII). The focal person, BDRRMC officers, ACDVs and partner CSOs shall attend anticipatory-action training when OCD Region VIII cascades the IRR. This is a new mandatory item and currently scores 0.\n\n## 7.2 Administration and Training Division\n\nThe Administration and Training Division provides the administrative and organizational support required for a dependable CDRRMO. It shall perform the following functions, subject to the approved division mandate and coordination with HRMO and other competent offices:\n\n- Prepare and maintain administrative work plans, office records, correspondence, internal directives, schedules and document-control registers.\n\n- Coordinate with HRMO on staffing records, personnel movement, attendance, leave, training nominations, performance documentation and position-description updates.\n\n- Support CDRRMC Secretariat administration when assigned, including notices, agendas, attendance, minutes, resolutions and action monitoring.\n\n- Maintain the CDRRMO''s inventory of office equipment, supplies, vehicles, communications assets and administrative resources in coordination with property and supply offices.\n\n- Coordinate training needs assessment, annual training plans, training records, competency matrices and post-training evaluation.\n\n- Maintain staff induction materials, orientation checklists, internal administrative SOPs and handover records.\n\n- Coordinate personnel welfare and administrative support during extended operations, including rest, meals, transport and family-contact arrangements as approved.\n\n- Support budget preparation, procurement planning, records retention and audit readiness in coordination with Budget, Accounting, BAC and Records Management.\n\nThe Division shall not assume the statutory personnel authority of HRMO, the financial authority of the Budget and Accounting Offices, or procurement authority assigned to the BAC and authorized procurement officials.\n\n## 7.3 Research and Planning Division\n\nThe Research and Planning Division provides the analytical and planning functions that enable the City to understand risk, prioritize interventions and integrate DRRM and climate resilience into development decisions. It shall:\n\n- Coordinate preparation, review and updating of the Local DRRM Plan and relevant contingency, preparedness, mitigation and recovery plans.\n\n- Maintain technical linkages with the CDRA, CLUP, CDP, LCCAP, public service continuity plans and sectoral plans, ensuring that versions, assumptions and data sources are traceable.\n\n- Coordinate with CPDO, GIS/ICT and sector offices on hazard, exposure, vulnerability, capacity and critical-facility information, without displacing their technical mandates.\n\n- Develop and maintain risk-information products, planning assumptions, scenario analyses and evidence briefs for CDRRMC and executive decision-making.\n\n- Support pre-disaster risk assessment and anticipatory-action planning with Operations and Warning and relevant sector offices.\n\n- Prepare planning inputs for annual investment programming, DRRM financing proposals, project screening and resource prioritization.\n\n- Maintain planning indicators, monitoring frameworks, data dictionaries, evidence registers and knowledge products.\n\n- Coordinate technical studies, research partnerships, lessons analysis and integration of climate and disaster risk into local policy.\n\n- Ensure that data collection and sharing comply with privacy, confidentiality, records and information-security requirements.\n\nThe Division shall distinguish technical data stewardship from policy approval. CPDO remains the competent office for its statutory and assigned development-planning functions; engineering, health, environment and other technical offices retain their respective professional and regulatory mandates.\n\n## 7.4 Operations and Warning Division\n\nThe Operations and Warning Division maintains the CDRRMO''s operational readiness and coordinates warning, preparedness and response-support functions in accordance with approved plans and the separate EOC and ICS procedures. It shall:\n\n- Maintain routine hazard monitoring and warning coordination arrangements with PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and other competent sources.\n\n- Translate official warnings and risk information into operational recommendations and warning-action coordination with barangays and sector offices.\n\n- Maintain duty officer arrangements, operational contact directories, readiness checklists and escalation protocols.\n\n- Coordinate preparedness activities, drills, exercises and operational readiness reviews with BDRRMCs and response agencies.\n\n- Support pre-disaster risk assessment, anticipatory action planning, prepositioning and evacuation recommendations under the applicable legal and operational procedures.\n\n- Coordinate response information and resource requests through the activated EOC and designated incident management arrangements.\n\n- Support the CDRRMO''s coordination of response clusters and technical working arrangements without assuming the command authority of the Incident Commander or mandates of response agencies.\n\n- Maintain operational logs, warning dissemination records, deployment coordination records and after-action inputs.\n\n- Coordinate operational transition and technical inputs to recovery and rehabilitation functions.\n\nThe Division''s internal duty officer system shall operate during normal periods and heightened readiness. Its activation and shift arrangements shall be aligned with the EOC procedures in the relevant chapters and shall not be used to imply that the full EOC is continuously activated.\n\n![Figure 9: 7.4 Operations and Warning Division](/library/ch2v12/fig-09.webp)\n\n# 8. Staffing Establishment, Plantilla and Workforce Planning\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a staffing complement that is lawful, functionally sufficient, competency-based and sustainable. Staffing decisions shall be anchored in the statutory functions of the office, JMC No. 2014-1, the City''s approved organizational structure and staffing pattern, CSC qualification standards, DBM requirements and available appropriations.\n\n![Figure 10: 8. Staffing Establishment, Plantilla and Workforce Planning](/library/ch2v12/fig-10.webp)\n\n## 8.1 Staffing Establishment Principles\n\n- Mandatory office functions and minimum positions shall be identified first, before proposing additional or specialized roles.\n\n- Each proposed position shall have a documented justification linking workload, risk exposure, service requirements, outputs and required competencies.\n\n- Positions shall be created, reclassified, filled or redesigned only through the prescribed HRMO, Budget, DBM/CSC and local approval processes.\n\n- Critical functions shall have designated alternates or continuity arrangements to reduce single-person dependency.\n\n- Temporary, project-based, volunteer or partner personnel shall not be treated as substitutes for mandatory plantilla positions where law requires permanent staffing.\n\n- Staffing proposals shall account for 24/7 operational requirements, leave, training, fatigue, field deployment and emergency surge demands.\n\n## 8.2 Workforce and Plantilla Review\n\nThe CDRRMO Head, in coordination with HRMO and Budget, shall conduct an annual workforce review. The review shall compare the approved plantilla with required functions, filled and vacant positions, qualifications, workload, duty coverage, training and operational gaps.\n\n| **Review dimension**      | **Minimum information / evidence**                                                                              |\n|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Approved staffing pattern | Latest approved organizational chart, plantilla and relevant DBM/CSC approvals.                                 |\n| Position status           | Authorized, filled, vacant, temporarily assigned, detailed or otherwise unavailable.                            |\n| Qualification compliance  | Position qualification standards, appointment records and supporting credentials, subject to HRMO verification. |\n| Workload and coverage     | Work plans, task distribution, duty rosters, leave coverage, incident deployments and overtime records.         |\n| Competency profile        | Required versus demonstrated competencies, training and certification records.                                  |\n| Continuity risks          | Single-person dependencies, absence of alternates, succession gaps and critical knowledge held by individuals.  |\n| Resource implications     | Personnel services costs, training, PPE, insurance and operational support requirements.                        |\n\n## 8.3 Minimum Staffing and Gap Analysis\n\nA staffing gap analysis shall be prepared against the applicable JMC No. 2014-1 requirements and the current approved staffing pattern. The analysis shall not assume a fixed numerical complement without verifying the current standard and its applicability to Ormoc City. It shall identify: (a) required positions; (b) authorized positions; (c) filled positions; (d) vacancies; (e) qualification gaps; (f) operational coverage gaps; and (g) proposed corrective actions.\n\nThe assessment workbook''s A2.3 indicator requires evidence of minimum plantilla positions and the ratio of filled to required positions. The CDRRMO and HRMO shall reconcile the indicator against the approved plantilla and current national guidance before using it as a compliance conclusion.\n\n## 8.4 Position Creation and Approval Workflow\n\n1.  CDRRMO Head documents the functional need, workload evidence, proposed duties, competency requirements and expected outputs.\n\n2.  HRMO reviews the proposed position, qualification standards, organizational fit and personnel implications.\n\n3.  The Budget Office reviews personnel-services affordability and funding implications.\n\n4.  City Legal Office reviews legal authority and consistency with existing ordinances and organizational issuances where required.\n\n5.  The proposal is routed through the City''s authorized executive and legislative processes and to DBM/CSC or other competent authorities as applicable.\n\n6.  Only after required approvals are secured shall the position be incorporated into the approved staffing pattern and recruitment or appointment process.\n\n# 9. Position Descriptions, Authority and Accountability\n\nEvery approved CDRRMO position shall have a current position description or job description consistent with CSC and City HR requirements. The description shall define the position''s purpose, major duties, decision authority, reporting relationship, qualifications, competencies, outputs and performance measures.\n\n## 9.1 Minimum contents of position descriptions\n\n| **Field**                         | **Required content**                                                                                           |\n|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Position title and item number    | Official title, salary grade where applicable, plantilla item and employment status.                           |\n| Organizational placement          | Office, division or unit; immediate supervisor; positions supervised.                                          |\n| Position purpose                  | Concise statement of why the position exists and the institutional result it supports.                         |\n| Major duties and responsibilities | Specific functions, frequency or context, expected outputs and coordination duties.                            |\n| Authority and limits              | Decisions the position may make; approvals required; matters reserved to superior or statutory authority.      |\n| Qualifications                    | Education, experience, training, eligibility, licenses or other requirements under applicable standards.       |\n| Competencies                      | Technical, managerial, operational, digital, communication and behavioural competencies.                       |\n| Performance measures              | Output, quality, timeliness, compliance and service indicators linked to OPCR/IPCR or equivalent system.       |\n| Interfaces                        | Internal and external offices, agencies, barangays and partners with which the position routinely coordinates. |\n| Review and approval               | Preparer, HRMO review, approving authority, effective date and revision history.                               |\n\n## 9.2 Delegation and accountability\n\nDelegation shall be documented and limited to authority that may lawfully be delegated. It shall identify the delegating authority, delegate, scope, duration, conditions, reporting requirements and revocation mechanism. Delegation does not remove the delegating official''s accountability for oversight where provided by law.\n\nRACI matrices in this chapter allocate operational work but do not confer appointment authority, statutory decision rights, financial authority or professional licensing. Where a decision is reserved by law or City policy to a particular official or body, that authority prevails over a RACI designation.\n\n# 10. EOC Staffing Interface and Operational Augmentation\n\nThe EOC is an operational coordination mechanism supported by personnel designated from the CDRRMO and participating City offices, agencies and partners. It is not a permanent fourth division of the CDRRMO. Its incident-time configuration shall be determined by the EOC activation level, incident complexity, hazard profile and coordination requirements.\n\nThe EOC staffing model shall be elaborated in the dedicated EOC organization and activation chapters. For purposes of this chapter, the CDRRMO shall ensure that the permanent office maintains an EOC personnel readiness register and coordinates with HRMO and participating offices to identify trained personnel and alternates.\n\n| **EOC functional role (illustrative)** | **Staffing source / interface**                                                                        | **Chapter 2 control**                                                                 |\n|----------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| EOC Manager                            | Designated by competent authority; may be drawn from CDRRMO or another qualified official as approved. | Written designation, role profile, authority limits and alternate.                    |\n| Operations                             | CDRRMO Operations and Warning and relevant response-agency representatives.                            | Competency and role-specific job action sheet.                                        |\n| Planning / Information                 | CDRRMO Research and Planning, GIS/ICT and agency information focal persons.                            | Data access, reporting standards and information-quality requirements.                |\n| Logistics                              | CDRRMO support staff and relevant City supply, property, procurement and transport offices.            | Clear separation of operational request, procurement, custody and financial approval. |\n| Administration / Finance               | Authorized administrative, budget, accounting and HR personnel.                                        | Maintain lawful approval and transaction controls.                                    |\n| Agency / cluster liaison               | CSWD, CHO, Engineering, PNP, BFP, AFP, DepEd, utilities, Red Cross and other relevant partners.        | Written focal-person designation and alternate; agency retains its mandate.           |\n\n![Figure 11: 10. EOC Staffing Interface and Operational Augmentation](/library/ch2v12/fig-11.webp)\n\nThe Institutional Assessment B4 indicators require a defined EOC structure, pre-designated staff with alternates, duty rosters and handover protocols, agency/cluster liaison assignments, job action sheets and responder welfare. These are to be implemented through the EOC-specific chapters and annexes; Chapter 2 establishes the workforce and designation controls that support them.\n\n## 10.1 EOC Staffing at Red Activation\n\nAt Red, the EOC needs about 25 seats a shift, or about 50 people over two 12-hour shifts, before cluster desks are counted. The CDRRMO''s non-field staff and the proposed cluster coordinators cover most of this. The City shall pre-designate 15 to 20 trained augmentees from City offices to cover the rest, plus leave, fatigue and field deployment (SOP HR-06; Annex 2-D).\n\n**Figure 2.4**\n\n*People Needed to Staff the EOC at Red Compared With Available Sources*\n\n![Figure 12: 10.1 EOC Staffing at Red Activation](/library/ch2v12/fig-12.webp)\n\n*Note.* Estimates from the proposed EOC structure (Chapter 1, Figure 1.3). Field rescue staff are excluded because they deploy with the Incident Management Team.\n\n**Geospatial support.** The EOC Planning Section shall include a GIS and information-management function that keeps the geospatial common operating picture during activation. In normal operations, the GIS and data post in Research and Planning maintains the EOC reference point, map layers and offline map packages. Staff assigned to this function shall be trained in incident georeferencing and map-product control (section 12). The procedure itself is SOP 6-GIS-01 in the EOC Module.\n\n## 10.2 Cluster-Based Barangay DRRM Coordinators (Proposal for Validation)\n\nA proposal developed within the CDRRMO would assign six coordinators to clusters of barangays, about 14 barangays each, covering all 85 barangays. It answers gaps no other plan covers: there is no barangay-to-EOC reporting flow (B5.2 = 1), no drill yet shows purok welfare checks within 12 hours (A7.9 = 1), and BDRRMC functionality has not been validated across all barangays (A7.3). It builds on national tools the City already uses: Operation L!sto, the harmonized SitRep (RDRRMC VIII Res. No. 12-006) and RDANA. The assessment supports the proposal in principle, with the conditions in Table 2.4.\n\n**Figure 2.5**\n\n*Hazard Tags Recorded in the Cluster Proposal, by Cluster*\n\n![Figure 13: 10.2 Cluster-Based Barangay DRRM Coordinators (Proposal for Validation)](/library/ch2v12/fig-13.webp)\n\n*Note.* Numbers in brackets are barangays per cluster. Tag counts exceed barangay counts where a barangay carries two tags (San Juan, Linao, Naungan and Punta: flood and storm surge). Thirty-five barangays carry no tag yet and need tags from the updated CDRA.\n\n**Table 2.4**\n\n*Conditions for Adopting the Cluster-Based Coordinator Model*\n\n| **\\#** | **Area**             | **Condition**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n|--------|----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1      | Cluster balance      | Clusters range from 6 to 18 barangays. Add a cluster map and balance clusters by population, hazard and travel time, not by count. One option: move Lao and San Juan from District 2 to District 3, making one west-coast cluster of eight with shared coastal exposure. |\n| 2      | Hazard tagging       | Tag every barangay from one hazard register, the updated CDRA. Reconcile the tags with the DRRM 10-Year Plan''s 46 critical flood barangays and the LCCAP''s 73 flood-prone barangays.                                                                                     |\n| 3      | Reporting line       | During warning and response, coordinators report to the EOC Planning Section (situation unit), keeping one channel and the DILG VIII reporting cut-offs. In normal operations they report to Research and Planning.                                                      |\n| 4      | Evacuation decisions | Coordinators advise. The decision stays with the Punong Barangay or the Mayor under the evacuation ordinance (A1.5).                                                                                                                                                     |\n| 5      | Barangay DRRM funds  | Each barangay''s BDRRMF is approved by its Sangguniang Barangay; coordinators give technical help only, as the field secretariat of the BDRRMP Review Team (EO No. 2023-0085).                                                                                            |\n| 6      | Employment and cost  | State plantilla or contract of service, the annual cost and the fund source. Check with the Budget Office whether LDRRMF use for salaries is allowed (JMC 2013-1, COA rules). Contract staff cannot hold supervisory or accountable roles.                               |\n| 7      | Approval route       | The SP committee chair recommends approval; the Mayor approves; new posts go through the SP.                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| 8      | Measures             | Adopt five measures: % of BDRRMPs certified; % of barangays with a completed Operation L!sto checklist by 1 June; barangay report timeliness against SitRep cut-offs; % of puroks accounted for within 12 hours in drills; % of barangays with a trained response team.  |\n\n*Note.* Conditions proposed for validation at the December 2026 workshop. Two citations in the proposal shall be checked before it goes to the SP: RA 10121 Sec. 12(a) and the DILG circular for Operation L!sto.\n\n**Where coordinators sit.** In normal operations, coordinators sit in Research and Planning for plan review, data and L!sto checks, and work with Administration and Training on training content. On EOC activation they become the Barangay Liaison Desk under the Planning Section, one desk per cluster. The coordinator for the eastern upland cluster shall be able to report by radio or satellite, because that cluster can be cut off.\n\n**Pilot.** Run two clusters for one typhoon season, June to November 2027: District 1 (eastern uplands, isolation risk) and District 3 (west coast, storm-surge risk). Measure them against condition 8, then scale to six clusters through the FY2028 budget.\n\n# 11. Duty Systems, Availability and Continuity of Personnel\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a duty system proportionate to its legal functions, operational requirements and approved staffing. The system shall distinguish routine office hours, duty officer coverage, heightened readiness and full EOC activation. It shall not assume continuous full EOC activation.\n\n## 11.1 Duty roster and handover\n\nA controlled duty roster shall identify primary personnel, alternates, contact details, duty period, supervisor, escalation route and any special competency or authorization required. Handover shall cover current warnings, incidents, pending actions, resource requests, equipment status, communications issues, unresolved decisions and records requiring follow-up.\n\n**Current practice (site visit, October 2026).** The CDRRMO already runs 24-hour duty from the CDRRM Office for three priority areas: City Traffic Monitoring, Public Safety and Emergency Monitoring. To move A2.5 and B4.3 from 2 to 3, the CDRRMO shall: (a) have the White-level OpCen SOP approved; (b) give each monitoring stream a duty post, a task list and a reporting line, including who escalates to the CDRRMO Head and when; and (c) keep signed rosters and handover forms (Annex 2-E) for at least three months as proof of use.\n\n## 11.2 Continuity and succession\n\n- Maintain at least one trained alternate for critical CDRRMO functions and ensure EOC key positions are designated with alternates in accordance with the applicable EOC standard.\n\n- Maintain updated contact and emergency notification lists with access controls.\n\n- Document acting arrangements and succession in accordance with City rules.\n\n- Ensure essential records, credentials and operating instructions are accessible to authorized alternates.\n\n- Include staff absence, facility disruption, ICT outage and simultaneous incidents in continuity exercises.\n\n- Coordinate Public Service Continuity Planning for CDRRMO functions with the City''s designated continuity focal office.\n\n## 11.3 Fatigue management\n\nDuty schedules shall provide reasonable rest periods, relief arrangements, meals, hydration and safe transport where required. Supervisors shall monitor fatigue and fitness for duty, particularly during prolonged operations. The EOC''s shift cycle and rest arrangements shall be specified in its dedicated operational procedures.\n\n![Figure 14: 11.3 Fatigue management](/library/ch2v12/fig-14.webp)\n\n# 12. Competency Development and Professionalization\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a competency-based human-resource development system. Training shall be driven by role requirements, risk exposure, performance findings and the City''s operational priorities rather than by attendance targets alone.\n\n## 12.1 Competency framework\n\n| **Competency group**         | **Indicative competencies**                                                                                                                          |\n|------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Institutional and governance | RA 10121 and local DRRM governance; planning; policy coordination; public administration; records and accountability.                                |\n| Risk and planning            | Hazard and risk assessment; CDRA interpretation; contingency planning; climate adaptation; PDRA; recovery planning.                                  |\n| Operational                  | ICS; EOC operations; emergency coordination; warning dissemination; evacuation coordination; resource request and tracking.                          |\n| Technical and field          | First aid / basic life support; search and rescue or water safety where relevant; damage assessment; safety and incident awareness, subject to role. |\n| Information and digital      | GIS; data management; situation reporting; dashboard use; cybersecurity; privacy and information security.                                           |\n| Leadership and people        | Supervision; facilitation; negotiation; decision-making under pressure; inclusion; conflict sensitivity; staff welfare.                              |\n| Continuity and improvement   | Public service continuity; business impact analysis; exercise design; after-action review; corrective action and quality improvement.                |\n\n## 12.2 Training needs assessment and annual plan\n\n1.  Map each approved position to required competencies and proficiency levels.\n\n2.  Assess current competence using records, supervisor review, exercises, tests and demonstrated performance.\n\n3.  Identify priority gaps based on life-safety risk, legal requirements, operational criticality and current assessment findings.\n\n4.  Prepare an annual training and development plan with objectives, target personnel, provider, cost, schedule and evaluation method\n\n5.  Secure HRMO, Budget and approving-authority review and integrate the plan into the City''s personnel and financial planning cycles\n\n6.  Evaluate learning transfer through observed performance, exercises, work products and after-action reviews.\n\nTraining records shall include course title, provider, dates, participant, competency addressed, certificate or evidence, assessment result and renewal requirement where applicable. Attendance alone shall not be treated as proof of operational competence.\n\n![Figure 15: 12.2 Training needs assessment and annual plan](/library/ch2v12/fig-15.webp)\n\n# 13. Personnel Welfare, Occupational Safety and Psychosocial Support\n\nThe City shall apply a duty-of-care approach to CDRRMO personnel and responders. Personnel shall not be assigned to tasks beyond their competence, authorization or safe operating conditions. Safety and welfare arrangements shall be coordinated with HRMO, occupational safety and health focal persons, health services and other competent offices.\n\n## 13.1 Minimum welfare and safety controls\n\n- Documented hazard identification and risk assessment for office, field and emergency assignments.\n\n- Appropriate PPE, safety equipment, communications and task-specific protective measures.\n\n- Pre-deployment briefing, fitness-for-duty checks where appropriate, buddy arrangements and accountability procedures.\n\n- Access to first aid, medical referral, insurance and applicable benefits, subject to law and approved City policy.\n\n- Rest cycles, meals, hydration, sanitation and safe accommodation or transport during extended operations.\n\n- Psychological first aid, psychosocial support, referral pathways and post-incident support, consistent with RA 11036 and applicable protocols.\n\n- Protection against harassment, discrimination, retaliation and unsafe working conditions.\n\n- Family communication and emergency contact arrangements consistent with privacy safeguards.\n\n- Post-deployment check-in, incident reporting, injury/near-miss reporting and learning review.\n\n**Insurance and welfare evidence (A2.7).** CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03 and 04 s.2022 (31 March 2022) adopted NDRRMC MC No. 64 s.2021 on the accreditation, mobilization and protection of community disaster volunteers, and approved a risk categorization as the basis for buying insurance for accredited volunteers. No policy or proof of purchase was sighted, and no equivalent arrangement covers regular CDRRMO staff (insurance, hazard pay, PPE issue, MHPSS). The responder welfare policy shall close both gaps.\n\n![Figure 16: 13.1 Minimum welfare and safety controls](/library/ch2v12/fig-16.webp)\n\n## 13.2 Inclusion and reasonable accommodation\n\nPersonnel arrangements shall respect gender equality, disability inclusion, age-related needs, cultural and language diversity and reasonable accommodation. Field assignments, facilities, training and communications shall be designed to enable qualified personnel to participate safely and effectively.\n\n## 13.3 Responder welfare ownership\n\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate the welfare framework but shall not displace HRMO, CHO, occupational safety officers or other competent offices. Each deploying office remains responsible for the personnel under its administrative control, subject to incident coordination and applicable law.\n\n# 14. Human Resource Administration, Performance and Conduct\n\nAll CDRRMO personnel remain subject to the City''s approved personnel administration systems and applicable CSC rules. The CDRRMO Head and division chiefs shall align work plans and individual performance commitments with the CDRRMO mandate, approved office targets and City performance management requirements.\n\n## 14.1 Performance management\n\n- Translate annual office outputs into division and individual commitments.\n\n- Use measurable indicators covering timeliness, quality, compliance, coordination, readiness and service outcomes.\n\n- Conduct regular supervisory discussions and document performance support or corrective measures.\n\n- Use after-action review findings and exercise results to identify training and process improvements.\n\n- Ensure that institutional assessment findings are used for system improvement and not as a substitute for formal individual performance evaluation.\n\n## 14.2 Conduct, confidentiality and information security\n\nPersonnel shall comply with applicable codes of conduct, confidentiality obligations, records rules, data privacy and cybersecurity policies. Access to beneficiary information, personnel files, health information, incident intelligence and sensitive operational records shall be role-based and limited to legitimate official purposes.\n\n## 14.3 Volunteers, surge personnel and partner staff\n\nVolunteers, accredited civil society partners, auxiliary personnel and surge staff may augment capacity where legally permitted and appropriately managed. Their roles, supervision, safety, training, insurance, access to information and limits of authority shall be documented. They shall not be treated as substitutes for required permanent positions or authorized officials.\n\nUnder CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03 and 04 s.2022, the City applies NDRRMC MC No. 64 s.2021 to accredited community disaster volunteers (ACDVs). SP Res. No. 2023-347 authorizes a MOA with the CPR-RIMT Rapid Rescue Rider Ormoc Chapter for volunteer augmentation. To close A2.8, the CDRRMO shall file the signed MOA; keep an ACDV registry with accreditation, insurance and deployment rules; route volunteer tasking through the EOC during activation; and add anticipatory-action tasks for ACDVs (RA 12287 IRR Rule IV Sec. 5).\n\n# 15. Workforce Governance Workflows\n\n## 15.1 Annual workforce planning and staffing review\n\n1.  CDRRMO Head issues the annual workforce review instruction and confirms the review period.\n\n2.  Division chiefs submit workload, outputs, staffing, competency, duty coverage and continuity information.\n\n3.  Administration and Training consolidates the workforce profile and evidence register.\n\n4.  HRMO validates positions, qualification standards, appointment status and personnel implications.\n\n5.  The Budget Office assesses personnel-services and training affordability.\n\n6.  CDRRMO Head approves the office-level workforce gap analysis for submission through the City''s authorized process.\n\n7.  Corrective actions are assigned, monitored and reviewed quarterly.\n\n## 15.2 Recruitment, appointment and onboarding interface\n\n1.  CDRRMO identifies an approved vacant position and documents operational requirements.\n\n2.  HRMO confirms vacancy status, qualification standards, recruitment and selection requirements.\n\n3.  Budget confirms funding and personnel-services availability.\n\n4.  Recruitment, selection and appointment proceed through the City''s authorized process and CSC rules.\n\n5.  Upon appointment or assignment, the supervisor provides an induction briefing, position description, work plan, records and access orientation, safety briefing and training needs assessment.\n\n6.  Completion of onboarding is documented and retained in the appropriate personnel and office records.\n\n## 15.3 Training needs assessment and development cycle\n\n1.  Map position competencies and assess current proficiency.\n\n2.  Identify gaps using performance, incident, exercise and assessment evidence.\n\n3.  Prioritize training by legal requirement, risk and operational criticality.\n\n4.  Prepare and approve the annual training plan and budget.\n\n5.  Deliver training and maintain verifiable records.\n\n6.  Evaluate application in work performance or exercises and update the competency matrix.\n\n## 15.4 EOC personnel designation and readiness\n\n1.  CDRRMO Head identifies permanent office personnel who may be assigned to EOC roles, in consultation with division chiefs.\n\n2.  Participating City offices and partner agencies nominate qualified primary and alternate liaison personnel.\n\n3.  HRMO and the relevant office head confirm administrative status and availability.\n\n4.  CDRRMO maintains the controlled roster and records relevant training and competencies.\n\n5.  On activation, personnel report under the approved EOC activation and staffing procedure.\n\n6.  After deactivation, personnel return to their normal assignments and submit handover, records and welfare reports as required.\n\n## 15.5 Personnel welfare incident and corrective action\n\n1.  Personnel report injury, exposure, near miss, fatigue concern or psychosocial support need through the designated channel.\n\n2.  Supervisor ensures immediate safety, medical response or referral and notification of the competent office.\n\n3.  HRMO / occupational safety / health focal persons record and assess the incident under applicable rules.\n\n4.  CDRRMO reviews operational implications and identifies corrective actions.\n\n5.  Actions are assigned, tracked and verified for effectiveness without disclosing protected personal information.\n\n# 2.16 RACI and Coordination Matrices\n\nThe following matrices allocate operational responsibility. They are subject to the statutory authority, approved organizational structure, personnel rules and formal delegations described in Chapter 1. One accountable owner is identified for each routine process; where a decision belongs to a statutory or collective authority, that authority remains controlling.\n\n| **Process / activity**                                 | **Accountable (A)**                                               | **Responsible (R)**                                       | **Consulted (C) / Informed (I)**         |\n|--------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| Annual CDRRMO workforce plan                           | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Division chiefs; Administration and Training              | HRMO; Budget; Mayor                      |\n| Review of organizational structure                     | City Mayor / authorized approving authority                       | CDRRMO Head; HRMO                                         | CLO; Budget; DBM/CSC as applicable       |\n| Position description preparation                       | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Immediate supervisor / division chief                     | HRMO; CLO where needed                   |\n| Qualification standards and recruitment administration | HRMO / authorized appointing authority                            | HRMO                                                      | CDRRMO Head; Budget; concerned division  |\n| Annual training needs assessment                       | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Division chiefs; Administration and Training              | HRMO; OCD; training providers; Budget    |\n| Annual training plan and budget                        | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Administration and Training                               | HRMO; Budget; division chiefs            |\n| Duty roster and internal handover                      | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Operations and Warning / designated supervisor            | HRMO; division chiefs                    |\n| EOC primary and alternate nominations                  | Head of each participating office for its personnel               | Office focal persons                                      | CDRRMO; HRMO; EOC Manager                |\n| EOC roster consolidation                               | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Designated EOC readiness focal person                     | Participating offices; HRMO; OCD         |\n| Personnel safety and welfare system                    | City authority designated under City OSH / HR policy              | HRMO; CHO; occupational safety focal persons; supervisors | CDRRMO; Budget; partner agencies         |\n| Performance monitoring                                 | CDRRMO Head                                                       | Division chiefs / immediate supervisors                   | HRMO; City performance management office |\n| Workforce records and privacy controls                 | HRMO for official personnel files; CDRRMO for operational records | Authorized records custodians                             | DPO; ICT; CLO                            |\n\n## 2.16.1 Functional interface matrix\n\n| **Function**                   | **Lead division**                               | **Key supporting offices / partners**                        | **Boundary / coordination rule**                                                              |\n|--------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Personnel administration       | Administration and Training                     | HRMO; Budget; Accounting                                     | CDRRMO coordinates; HRMO retains personnel administration authority.                          |\n| DRRM and climate-risk planning | Research and Planning                           | CPDO; CENRO; Engineering; sector offices; academe            | CDRRMO supplies DRRM technical inputs; each office retains its planning or technical mandate. |\n| Hazard monitoring and warning  | Operations and Warning                          | PAGASA; PHIVOLCS; BDRRMCs; communications providers          | Use official warning sources; issuing agencies retain authority over technical advisories.    |\n| EOC readiness                  | CDRRMO Head / designated readiness focal person | HRMO; IT; Engineering; utilities; participating offices; OCD | Maintain roster and readiness; detailed activation and EOC management are in later chapters.  |\n| Training and exercises         | Administration and Training                     | OCD; HRMO; BFP; PNP; CHO; training institutions              | Training plan is competency-based; agencies retain professional standards.                    |\n| Responder welfare              | Designated City HR / OSH / health authority     | HRMO; CHO; supervisors; insurance providers                  | Each deploying office retains duty of care for its personnel.                                 |\n| Information and records        | Division owning the operational record          | ICT; Records Officer; DPO; CLO                               | Apply role-based access, retention and privacy requirements.                                  |\n\n## 2.16.2 Budget-Cycle RACI (Draft for Validation)\n\nStaff reflections asked for clearer roles in forming the LDRRMF investment plan. Planning and execution need different strengths, and the plan is strongest when both sign it. The RACI below gives Research and Planning the lead in formulation, keeps execution and audit controls with Administration and the City''s finance offices, and closes IAT gaps A4.4 and A10.4.\n\n**Table 2.5**\n\n*Budget-Cycle RACI for the LDRRMF Investment Plan*\n\n| **Step**                                                                                    | **Responsible**                           | **Accountable**        | **Consulted**                           | **Informed**          |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|------------------------|-----------------------------------------|-----------------------|\n| 1\\. Baseline: CDRA, LDRRMP results, last year''s utilization                                 | Research and Planning                     | CDRRMO Head            | Operations and Warning; CPDO            | CDRRMC                |\n| 2\\. Division needs and proposals                                                            | Each division                             | CDRRMO Head            | Research and Planning                   | —                     |\n| 3\\. Draft LDRRMFIP: prioritized; tagged to LDRRMP/CDP PPA and thematic area; 70/30; AA line | Research and Planning                     | CDRRMO Head            | Administration (costing); Budget Office | —                     |\n| 4\\. Costing, PPMP/APP and procurement plan                                                  | Administration                            | CDRRMO Head            | Research and Planning                   | BAC                   |\n| 5\\. CDRRMC approval, then SP adoption                                                       | Secretariat                               | CDRRMC; SP             | Budget Office                           | DILG; OCD VIII        |\n| 6\\. Execution: obligation, procurement, disbursement                                        | Administration                            | CDRRMO Head            | Budget Office; Accountant; BAC          | Research and Planning |\n| 7\\. Quarterly report: results and finances                                                  | Research and Planning with Administration | CDRRMO Head            | —                                       | CDRRMC                |\n| 8\\. Realignment or reallocation                                                             | Research and Planning                     | CDRRMC; SP concurrence | Administration; Accountant              | Budget Office         |\n\n*Note.* R = does the work; A = accountable; C = consulted; I = informed. Draft for validation at the 1–4 December 2026 workshop.\n\n# 17. Performance Standards, Records and Quality Assurance\n\n![Figure 17: 17. Performance Standards, Records and Quality Assurance](/library/ch2v12/fig-17.webp)\n\nThe CDRRMO shall apply a Plan–Do–Check–Act (PDCA) approach to organizational and human-resource management. The objective is to ensure that the structure is not only approved on paper but functions reliably and improves through evidence.\n\n| **PDCA stage** | **Organizational / HR application**                                                                        | **Minimum evidence**                                                                        |\n|----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Plan           | Review mandates, workload, staffing, competencies, duty coverage and welfare risks; set annual objectives. | Approved organogram; plantilla review; workforce plan; competency matrix; training plan.    |\n| Do             | Implement staffing, supervision, training, duty systems, designation and welfare controls.                 | Position descriptions; designation orders; rosters; training records; handover records.     |\n| Check          | Review outputs, vacancies, qualifications, response readiness, exercise performance and staff welfare.     | OPCR/IPCR or equivalent; attendance; audit findings; exercise evaluations; welfare reports. |\n| Act            | Correct staffing, competency, process and continuity gaps; revise position descriptions and SOPs.          | Corrective action register; revised plans; approved changes; follow-up verification.        |\n\n## 17.1 Core workforce performance indicators\n\n| **Indicator**                 | **Suggested measure / evidence**                                                                 | **Review frequency**            |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_v1.2.md (Chapter 2, v1.2 working draft (IAT v1.5))','draft')
